  "response": "J Harlen Bretz published his first papers on the Channeled Scabland in 1923. Bretz's flood interpretation was accepted by the geological community immediately after his 1923 publications. Yet Bretz's critics attributed the scablands to slow erosion by ordinary rivers and glacial meltwater, and a discipline that greeted the flood reading with instant assent cannot also have been a discipline arguing for patient fluvial and meltwater work: the two pictures of the reception cannot both be true. Whereas the critics insisted on gradual excavation by ordinary rivers and meltwater, Bretz defended his flood hypothesis before the Geological Society of Washington in January 1927, four years after those first papers had appeared.\n\nThe weakness of the flood case, as its opponents pressed it, was the absence of a reservoir large enough to deliver the water, and that weakness was remedied only in the decades following the 1927 defense. Pardee published evidence of giant current ripples on the floor of Glacial Lake Missoula in 1942, and that published evidence shows that Joseph Thomas Pardee's identification of Glacial Lake Missoula as the source of the floodwaters was correct.\n\nThe glacier responsible for holding that lake back has been identified in mutually exclusive ways. The Okanogan lobe of the ice sheet dammed the Clark Fork River to impound Glacial Lake Missoula. Assigning the barrier to that lobe cannot be squared with the account of the damming ice set out next; at most one of the two placements can stand. The Purcell Trench lobe of the Cordilleran ice sheet dammed the Clark Fork River, and that blockage caused Glacial Lake Missoula to impound roughly 2,000 cubic kilometers of water. The release of that stored volume produced a peak flood discharge through the scablands estimated at about 10 million cubic meters per second.\n\nErratic boulders stranded hundreds of feet above valley floors record the depth of the floodwaters, and their perched positions high on the valley walls confirm that water passed through the channels at the estimated peak rate. Rhythmite layers in the Touchet beds of the Walla Walla valley record dozens of separate flood pulses.\n\nThose boulders, left far above any level a river could reach, show that Bretz's catastrophic flood hypothesis was vindicated; in other words, the scientific community came to accept Bretz's flood origin for the Channeled Scablands. The adjudication was in the end geomorphic rather than rhetorical, decided by quantities that only a flood of the inferred scale could have produced.",
